        "Face the chapter's closing question: why do we make mistakes when judging the feelings, personalities, intentions, lies, and truths of conspecifics? Weigh the Gibsonian affordances answer (error is overgeneralization), the lens model answer (probabilistic sloppiness), and the behavioral ecology answer (imprecision is strategic, since being a hard read has evolutionary advantage), then issue a verdict on the future of error research armed with the tools of the computer-vision era.",
